Box 4.12. Impacts of fox and cat predation in Australia
Multiple studies have established that Felis catus (cat) and Vulpes vulpes (red fox) have had particularly significant impacts on, and continue to threaten, many native Australian vertebrate species . Vulpes vulpes has been shown to suppress populations of Petrogale lateralis (black-footed rock-wallaby; ; Figure 4.24), Dasyurus geoffroii (western quoll; , ground-dwelling and arboreal mammals , medium-sized marsupials , and even large species such as Macropus giganteus (eastern grey kangaroo; . When not controlled, Vulpes vulpes also reduce abundance of Varanus gouldii (sand goanna), diurnal scincid lizards , and Varanus varius (lace monitor; , and can destroy turtle nests, severely impacting their populations . Vulpes vulpes have also been implicated in colonial seabird , Leipoa ocellata (malleefowl; , and ground-foraging passerine declines .
Felis catus is currently considered the single most significant threat to Australian mammals . Indeed, Felis catus has been implicated in approximately two thirds of Australian native mammal extinctions, and another 54 native mammal taxa have suffered severe range contractions and are seriously threatened by cat predation. Felis catus caused local extirpation of a native rodent, Rattus villosissimus (long-haired rat) in a Northern Territory tropical savanna , and has been identified as a factor contributing to northern Australian mammal declines . Davies  demonstrated that Felis catus predation on threatened Conilurus penicillatus (brush?tailed rabbit?rat) is driving the remnant population to extinction on Melville Island, suggesting that predation has likely been a significant driver of Conilurus penicillatus decline throughout northern Australia (Figure 4.24). Additionally, predation of juvenile Dasyurus viverrinus (eastern quoll) by Felis catus is likely inhibiting recovery of low-density quoll populations across Tasmania .

Figure 4.24. Examples of native species with serious population declines due to invasive alien species. Petrogale lateralis (black-footed rock-wallaby, left), Leipoa ocellata (malleefowl, middle), Conilurus penicillatus (brush-tailed rabbit rat, right). Photo credit: Kym Nicolson, WM Commons - CC BY 4.0 (left) / butupa, WM Commons - CC BY 2.0 (middle) / Hugh Davies – CC BY 4.0 (right).
There is clear evidence to implicate predation by Felis catus in the loss of wildlife populations at a local and regional scale, but the contribution of Felis catus predation to Australian extinctions or extirpations is hard to disentangle from confounding other threats such as habitat clearance, changing fire regimes, and other feral vertebrates. On offshore islands, where confounding factors are less severe, Felis catus have been shown to decimate native fauna . Predation has caused the extinction of Cyanoramphus novaezelandiae erythroti (Macquarie Island Parakeet; , Traversia lyalli (Stephens Island wren; , and the extirpation from Marion Island of Pelecanoides urinatrix (common diving petrel; .
In addition to direct extinction of species, Felis catus predation can have significant knock-on effects at the ecosystem level, through alteration of ecosystem functioning. The local extinction of fossorial mammals (i.e., those that dig burrows underground), in Australian arid and semi-arid regions  has caused a loss of key soil-engineering processes, negatively impacting associated plant communities . 

